auxiliary_passages.statement_original:
Analogously to the population covariance matrix $\Gamma=(\langle g_j,g_k\rangle_{L^2})_{j,k\leq p}$, we define the empirical covariance matrix $\widehat\Gamma:=(\langle g_j,g_k\rangle_n)_{j,k\leq p}$. Using the same notation for partial matrices as in Assumption (CovB) and $X^{(J)}=(X_i^{(j)})_{i\leq n,j\in J}\in\mathbb R^{n\times|J|}$, the projections $\widehat\Pi_J$ and $\Pi_J$ can be written as
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat\Pi_J:&\ \mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^n,&\widehat\Pi_Jy&:=(X^{(J)})^\top\widehat\Gamma_J^{-1}\langle y,g_J\rangle_n,\\
\Pi_J:&\ L^2(P_{X_1})\to L^2(P_{X_1}),&\Pi_Jh&:=g_J^\top\Gamma_J^{-1}\langle h,g_J\rangle_{L^2}
\end{aligned}\tag{1.26}
\]
for $J\subset\{1,\ldots,p\}$.
